1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,915
This video is about ambiguous grammars. A grammar is ambiguous when one input string has two parse trees.

2
00:00:08,365 --> 00:00:31,085
We see why one tree matters. Then we remove ambiguity with precedence and associativity. Then the dangling else problem. At the end we solve exercises from the course notes and the short questions in questions-pre 12.3. The week 5 slides are not available yet, so this video uses the course notes, chapter 4.

3
00:00:31,535 --> 00:00:36,804
A parser reads an input string. It uses the grammar to build a parse tree.

4
00:00:37,254 --> 00:00:42,587
Here the grammar is: S goes to E, and E goes to E plus E, or to an integer.

5
00:00:43,037 --> 00:00:45,853
The input string is 1 plus 2 plus 3.

6
00:00:46,303 --> 00:00:50,655
One parse tree groups 1 plus 2 first. Then it adds 3.

7
00:00:51,105 --> 00:00:56,631
But the same grammar also allows a second tree. It groups 2 plus 3 first.

8
00:00:57,081 --> 00:01:00,899
So which tree does the parser give? The grammar does not say.

9
00:01:01,349 --> 00:01:09,541
The later phases of the compiler use the tree. If one string has two trees, the string can mean two different programs.

10
00:01:09,991 --> 00:01:21,490
Here is the definition from the course notes. A grammar is ambiguous if there is a legal input string with two different derivations, that is, two different parse trees.

11
00:01:21,940 --> 00:01:24,244
The test: take one input string.

12
00:01:24,694 --> 00:01:29,003
Do two derivations of it. Each derivation gives a parse tree.

13
00:01:29,453 --> 00:01:37,069
If both derivations accept, and they are different, the grammar is ambiguous. One example string is a proof.

14
00:01:37,519 --> 00:01:48,207
The hard part is to find that string. In theory this is undecidable. There are grammars for which no program can compute an ambiguity-revealing string.

15
00:01:48,657 --> 00:01:57,660
In practice, a little intuition is enough, for every grammar in this course. Look first at binary operators and at optional parts.

16
00:01:58,110 --> 00:02:04,275
Here is the grammar from the notes. S goes to E. E goes to E plus E, or to an integer.

17
00:02:04,725 --> 00:02:09,141
Is it ambiguous? Try the input string 1 plus 2 plus 3.

18
00:02:09,591 --> 00:02:13,495
Tree 1. Expand the top E with E goes to E plus E.

19
00:02:13,945 --> 00:02:15,673
The right E becomes 3.

20
00:02:16,123 --> 00:02:20,283
The left E is again E plus E, and it becomes 1 plus 2.

21
00:02:20,733 --> 00:02:24,915
So tree 1 groups the string as 1 plus 2, then plus 3.

22
00:02:25,365 --> 00:02:29,695
Tree 2. Expand the top E with E goes to E plus E again.

23
00:02:30,145 --> 00:02:32,471
This time the left E becomes 1.

24
00:02:32,921 --> 00:02:36,739
The right E is E plus E, and it becomes 2 plus 3.

25
00:02:37,189 --> 00:02:41,136
So tree 2 groups the string as 1 plus, 2 plus 3.

26
00:02:41,586 --> 00:02:46,045
One string has two different trees. So the grammar is ambiguous.

27
00:02:46,495 --> 00:02:50,527
Tree 1: 1 plus 2 is 3, and 3 plus 3 is 6.

28
00:02:50,977 --> 00:02:55,073
Tree 2: 2 plus 3 is 5, and 1 plus 5 is 6.

29
00:02:55,523 --> 00:03:05,229
Both values are 6, because addition is associative. But the grammar does not say if plus is left associative or right associative.

30
00:03:05,679 --> 00:03:15,450
Why do engineers remove ambiguity? The notes give two reasons. The table compares an ambiguous grammar with an L L one grammar.

31
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:30,684
Reason 1 is cost. An ambiguous context free grammar is not L L one. Its parser needs a nondeterministic pushdown automaton. An L L one grammar needs only a deterministic one.

32
00:03:31,134 --> 00:03:36,766
The algorithm is more complex: the Earley algorithm instead of recursive descent.

33
00:03:37,216 --> 00:03:41,333
And it takes more time: order n cubed instead of order n.

34
00:03:41,783 --> 00:03:51,063
Reason 2 is testing. For an ambiguous grammar, more than one tree is a correct output. With one correct tree, testing is easier.

35
00:03:51,513 --> 00:04:03,588
An ambiguous grammar is never L L one, and never a peg. Associativity and precedence are the usual sources of ambiguity, and we can design them out of the grammar.

36
00:04:04,038 --> 00:04:09,371
Add multiplication. E goes to E plus E, E times E, or an integer.

37
00:04:09,821 --> 00:04:12,317
Take the string 1 plus 2 times 3.

38
00:04:12,767 --> 00:04:18,591
Tree 1 puts plus lower in the tree. It groups 1 plus 2 first, then times 3.

39
00:04:19,041 --> 00:04:23,905
Tree 2 puts times lower in the tree. It groups 2 times 3 first.

40
00:04:24,355 --> 00:04:29,411
Tree 1: 1 plus 2 is 3, and 3 times 3 is 9. That is wrong.

41
00:04:29,861 --> 00:04:38,416
Tree 2: 2 times 3 is 6, and 1 plus 6 is 7. That is correct. The operator lower in the tree is done first.

42
00:04:38,866 --> 00:04:47,869
So now the ambiguity changes the result. The grammar must say that times comes before plus. This is operator precedence.

43
00:04:48,319 --> 00:05:00,969
A grammar must build in the order of operations. The notes give four rules. Rule a. Each level of precedence gets its own nonterminal: E for plus, and F for times.

44
00:05:01,419 --> 00:05:07,926
Rule b. The lowest level of precedence is the top level production. Here that is E, for plus.

45
00:05:08,376 --> 00:05:14,947
Rule c. Each production uses the next higher level. E uses F, the level for times.

46
00:05:15,397 --> 00:05:19,962
Rule d. The highest level includes the operands, the integers.

47
00:05:20,412 --> 00:05:29,692
The result: low precedence operators are near the root of the tree. High precedence operators are near the leaves, so they are done first.

48
00:05:30,142 --> 00:05:41,342
Now apply the rules to the old grammar, E plus E, E times E. Rule a. We have two operators, so two levels. Call them E for plus, and F for times.

49
00:05:41,792 --> 00:05:48,619
Rule d. F is the highest level, so F includes the operands. Add F goes to an integer.

50
00:05:49,069 --> 00:05:54,658
Rule b. E is the lowest level, so it stays at the top. Keep S goes to E.

51
00:05:55,108 --> 00:06:03,001
Rule c. Connect the levels. E goes to F. Now an E can become an F, but an F can never become a plus.

52
00:06:03,451 --> 00:06:13,990
A python check generated all strings up to length 7. The new grammar accepts exactly the same strings as the old one. Only the trees are different.

53
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:17,619
Parse 1 plus 2 times 3 with the new grammar.

54
00:06:18,069 --> 00:06:19,797
Start with S goes to E.

55
00:06:20,247 --> 00:06:25,473
The plus must come from E goes to E plus E. Only the E level has a plus.

56
00:06:25,923 --> 00:06:29,251
Each side becomes an F, with E goes to F.

57
00:06:29,701 --> 00:06:33,349
The left F becomes 1, with F goes to an integer.

58
00:06:33,799 --> 00:06:38,834
The right F is F times F. The times sits under F, near the leaves.

59
00:06:39,284 --> 00:06:41,545
The two lower F's become 2 and 3.

60
00:06:41,995 --> 00:06:53,195
So the only tree is 1 plus, 2 times 3. 2 times 3 is 6, and 1 plus 6 is 7. A python check found exactly one tree for this string.

61
00:06:53,645 --> 00:06:58,979
Is the new grammar unambiguous now? No. Try 4 plus 5 plus 6.

62
00:06:59,429 --> 00:07:04,847
The production E goes to E plus E still has an E on both sides of the plus.

63
00:07:05,297 --> 00:07:07,921
One tree groups 4 plus 5 first.

64
00:07:08,371 --> 00:07:11,017
The other tree groups 5 plus 6 first.

65
00:07:11,467 --> 00:07:16,117
Tree 1: 4 plus 5 is 9, and 9 plus 6 is 15.

66
00:07:16,567 --> 00:07:25,826
Tree 2: 5 plus 6 is 11, and 4 plus 11 is 15. Addition is associative, so here the value is the same.

67
00:07:26,276 --> 00:07:36,004
Precedence fixes the order between different operators. It does not fix the order when the same operator appears twice. That is associativity.

68
00:07:36,454 --> 00:07:42,641
Change the operators to minus and divide. For these, the grouping changes the value.

69
00:07:43,091 --> 00:07:45,437
Parse 8 minus 6 minus 2.

70
00:07:45,887 --> 00:07:51,071
One tree groups 6 minus 2 first. This is the right associative parse.

71
00:07:51,521 --> 00:07:56,983
The other tree groups 8 minus 6 first. This is the left associative parse.

72
00:07:57,433 --> 00:08:04,046
Right associative tree: 6 minus 2 is 4, and 8 minus 4 is 4. That is wrong.

73
00:08:04,496 --> 00:08:10,960
Left associative tree: 8 minus 6 is 2, and 2 minus 2 is 0. That is correct.

74
00:08:11,410 --> 00:08:18,898
Subtraction is left associative. x minus y minus z means x minus y, then minus z.

75
00:08:19,348 --> 00:08:25,001
The problem is E goes to E minus E. E can grow on either side of the minus.

76
00:08:25,451 --> 00:08:36,673
The fix. Change E goes to E minus E into E goes to E minus F. Now E appears only on the left of the minus. The rule is left recursive.

77
00:08:37,123 --> 00:08:42,904
Division is also left associative. So F goes to F divided by an integer.

78
00:08:43,354 --> 00:08:48,666
Here is the leftmost derivation of 8 minus 6 minus 2. Start with S.

79
00:08:49,116 --> 00:08:51,676
S becomes E, with S goes to E.

80
00:08:52,126 --> 00:08:57,289
E becomes E minus F. The orange symbol is the next one we expand.

81
00:08:57,739 --> 00:09:03,563
Expand the left E again: E minus F. Now we have E minus F minus F.

82
00:09:04,013 --> 00:09:08,471
The left E becomes F, because the only other choice adds a minus.

83
00:09:08,921 --> 00:09:13,721
The first F becomes 8. We always expand the leftmost nonterminal.

84
00:09:14,171 --> 00:09:15,899
The second F becomes 6.

85
00:09:16,349 --> 00:09:18,227
The last F becomes 2.

86
00:09:18,677 --> 00:09:29,130
The tree leans to the left. 8 minus 6 is done first: 8 minus 6 is 2, and 2 minus 2 is 0. A check found exactly one tree.

87
00:09:29,580 --> 00:09:38,561
Exponentiation is the opposite. It is right associative. x to the y to the z means x to the power, y to the z.

88
00:09:39,011 --> 00:09:45,305
So the rule is right recursive: E goes to F, caret, E. Only the right side can grow.

89
00:09:45,755 --> 00:09:53,371
For 2 caret 3 caret 2, the tree leans right. 3 caret 2 is lower in the tree, so it is done first.

90
00:09:53,821 --> 00:09:59,069
3 to the power 2 is 9, and 2 to the power 9 is 512.

91
00:09:59,519 --> 00:10:08,031
The other grouping gives 2 to the power 3, which is 8, and 8 to the power 2, which is 64. That is not this tree.

92
00:10:08,481 --> 00:10:17,825
The pattern: a left associative operator needs a left recursive rule. A right associative operator needs a right recursive rule.

93
00:10:18,275 --> 00:10:28,749
Now a problem. Left associativity needs left recursion. But an L L one grammar cannot have left recursion. Video 19 shows why.

94
00:10:29,199 --> 00:10:39,973
We can remove the left recursion with a tail rule: E goes to F T, and T goes to minus F T, or epsilon. This grammar is L L one.

95
00:10:40,423 --> 00:10:50,364
But look at the tree for 8 minus 6 minus 2. Now the tree leans to the right. Removing the left recursion also changed the associativity.

96
00:10:50,814 --> 00:11:02,889
The notes say there is no simple solution in B N F. But in E B N F we can use repetition: an Expr is a Term, followed by zero or more minus Term.

97
00:11:03,339 --> 00:11:07,136
The grammar gives a flat list. It does not fix the grouping.

98
00:11:07,586 --> 00:11:13,175
Then the parser implementation combines the list from the left. 8 minus 6 is 2.

99
00:11:13,625 --> 00:11:15,780
Then 2 minus 2 is 0.

100
00:11:16,230 --> 00:11:23,761
This E B N F grammar is weakly equivalent: it accepts the same strings, but it builds different trees.

101
00:11:24,211 --> 00:11:32,659
Now a classic ambiguity: the dangling else. This line gives the enrollment rules for E C E 351.

102
00:11:33,109 --> 00:11:38,293
If the department is E C E, and if the term is 3 A, enroll is yes.

103
00:11:38,743 --> 00:11:41,047
Else, enroll needs an override.

104
00:11:41,497 --> 00:11:47,961
Here is the typical grammar for an optional else. A conditional is: if, expression, statement.

105
00:11:48,411 --> 00:11:51,312
Or: if, expression, statement, else, statement.

106
00:11:51,762 --> 00:11:55,879
The else is optional, so the grammar has two conditional forms.

107
00:11:56,329 --> 00:12:06,121
Third year E C E students can enroll. But who needs an override? It depends on which if owns the else, the inner if or the outer if.

108
00:12:06,571 --> 00:12:15,937
This problem comes from Algol 60. Algol 60 gave us the B N F notation, and C is an industrial descendant of it.

109
00:12:16,387 --> 00:12:22,595
The same line can have two parse trees. The leaves of each tree read the line from left to right.

110
00:12:23,045 --> 00:12:26,565
In tree 1, the else belongs to the inner conditional.

111
00:12:27,015 --> 00:12:29,255
The outer conditional has no else.

112
00:12:29,705 --> 00:12:33,310
In tree 2, the else belongs to the outer conditional.

113
00:12:33,760 --> 00:12:35,957
The inner conditional has no else.

114
00:12:36,407 --> 00:12:44,386
Both trees accept the same line. So the grammar is ambiguous. A python check found exactly these two trees.

115
00:12:44,836 --> 00:12:51,151
The two trees are two different programs. Here is the program when the else belongs to the inner if.

116
00:12:51,601 --> 00:12:58,107
E C E students in another term take the no edge of the term test. They use an override.

117
00:12:58,557 --> 00:13:04,339
Students outside E C E take the no edge of the department test. They get nothing.

118
00:13:04,789 --> 00:13:08,095
Now the program when the else belongs to the outer if.

119
00:13:08,545 --> 00:13:12,343
Students from outside E C E use an override.

120
00:13:12,793 --> 00:13:15,950
E C E students in other terms get nothing.

121
00:13:16,400 --> 00:13:19,515
The same text gives two different enrollment rules.

122
00:13:19,965 --> 00:13:28,669
In practice there are four ways to resolve the dangling else. One: every if needs an end if. V H D L does this.

123
00:13:29,119 --> 00:13:32,276
Two: every if needs an else. Haskell does this.

124
00:13:32,726 --> 00:13:36,246
Three: put indentation into the grammar. Python does this.

125
00:13:36,696 --> 00:13:45,123
Four: add a note to the language specification. C and Java do this. The note says that the else goes with the inner if.

126
00:13:45,573 --> 00:13:54,554
Exercise 4.6.1 from the notes. Which of the four approaches change the grammar, so that the ambiguity is gone?

127
00:13:55,004 --> 00:13:56,647
Pause the video and try it.

128
00:13:57,097 --> 00:14:03,902
The answer is the first three. End if, a required else, and indentation all change the grammar.

129
00:14:04,352 --> 00:14:11,648
The note in the specification leaves the grammar ambiguous. The parser writer must deal with this case specially.

130
00:14:12,098 --> 00:14:23,767
Exercise 4.6.2. For the approaches that remove the ambiguity, write the new grammar. The notes do not give a solution yet, so this is our solution.

131
00:14:24,217 --> 00:14:25,860
Pause the video and try it.

132
00:14:26,310 --> 00:14:29,830
With end if, each conditional closes with end if.

133
00:14:30,280 --> 00:14:38,131
Now the programmer must write where the inner if ends. If end if comes after the else, the else is inside the inner if.

134
00:14:38,581 --> 00:14:42,506
If the end if comes first, the else belongs to the outer if.

135
00:14:42,956 --> 00:14:49,655
With a required else, there is only one form. Every if has its own else, so no else is left over.

136
00:14:50,105 --> 00:15:01,497
A python check counted the parse trees of every string up to 8 symbols. Both new grammars give at most one tree. The old grammar gives two trees for some strings.

137
00:15:01,947 --> 00:15:07,664
Indentation needs special tokens from the lexer, so we do not write that grammar here.

138
00:15:08,114 --> 00:15:29,063
Now exercises 4.11.6 and 4.11.7 from the notes. The grammar is E goes to E plus E, E times E, or int. First, give an input string with two parse trees. Second, refactor the grammar for normal precedence, and make it right associative.

139
00:15:29,513 --> 00:15:31,156
Pause the video and try it.

140
00:15:31,606 --> 00:15:36,619
Part 6. The string 1 plus 2 times 3 has two trees, as we saw.

141
00:15:37,069 --> 00:15:39,651
1 plus 2 plus 3 also works.

142
00:15:40,101 --> 00:15:47,695
Part 7. Our solution follows the notes. E goes to F plus E, or F. Plus is at the low level.

143
00:15:48,145 --> 00:15:52,753
F goes to int times F, or int. Times is at the high level.

144
00:15:53,203 --> 00:16:01,417
Both rules are right recursive. E is at the right end of F plus E, and F is at the right end of int times F.

145
00:16:01,867 --> 00:16:06,496
1 plus 2 times 3 has one tree: 1 plus, 2 times 3.

146
00:16:06,946 --> 00:16:14,050
1 plus 2 plus 3 also has one tree. It leans right, so the grammar is right associative.

147
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:28,815
The notes also show a wrong first try: E goes to E plus E or F, and F goes to F times F or int. The precedence is correct, but it is not right associative, and it is still ambiguous.

148
00:16:29,265 --> 00:16:37,137
A python check found at most one tree for every string up to length 7, and the same language as the original grammar.

149
00:16:37,587 --> 00:16:51,944
This grammar is not L L one, because of common prefixes: both E rules start with F, and both F rules start with int. That is OK here. Video 19 shows an L L one version.

150
00:16:52,394 --> 00:17:13,194
Exercise 4.11.4 from the notes. A to E are binary operators. Each has a different precedence, and none is associative. The input 3 C 4 B 1 A 2 D 6 B 7 E 5 gives this tree. List the operators from highest to lowest precedence.

151
00:17:13,644 --> 00:17:15,287
Pause the video and try it.

152
00:17:15,737 --> 00:17:21,475
An operator lower in the tree is done first. So it has higher precedence than its parent.

153
00:17:21,925 --> 00:17:24,997
C is under B. So C is higher than B.

154
00:17:25,447 --> 00:17:28,327
B is under A. So B is higher than A.

155
00:17:28,777 --> 00:17:31,657
D is under A. So D is higher than A.

156
00:17:32,107 --> 00:17:35,137
E is under D. So E is higher than D.

157
00:17:35,587 --> 00:17:39,107
The second B is under E. So B is higher than E.

158
00:17:39,557 --> 00:17:43,589
Chain the five facts: C, then B, then E, then D, then A.

159
00:17:44,039 --> 00:17:52,956
A python check tried all 120 orders of the five operators. Only C B E D A fits all five facts.

160
00:17:53,406 --> 00:18:03,859
Now the short questions in questions-pre, section 12.3. Question 12.3.4. How do we show that a grammar is ambiguous?

161
00:18:04,309 --> 00:18:08,213
Show one input string that has two parse trees in the grammar.

162
00:18:08,663 --> 00:18:23,362
Question 12.3.5. How do we show that a grammar is unambiguous? The posted answer: that is mathematically beyond this course. We say that we cannot find an input string with two parses.

163
00:18:23,812 --> 00:18:29,657
In this video, a python search over all short strings gave us extra confidence.

164
00:18:30,107 --> 00:18:36,145
Question 12.3.7. How many kinds of grammar equivalence did we see? Two.

165
00:18:36,595 --> 00:18:40,200
Weak equivalence: the grammars accept the same strings.

166
00:18:40,650 --> 00:18:45,557
Strong equivalence: the same strings, and exactly the same parse trees.

167
00:18:46,007 --> 00:18:53,153
Question 12.3.8. When we refactor a grammar, it is usually only weakly equivalent.

168
00:18:53,603 --> 00:19:00,771
For example, the precedence grammar accepts the same strings as the ambiguous one, but it builds different trees.

169
00:19:01,221 --> 00:19:11,333
The other questions in section 12.3 use this table from the notes, Figure 4.2. Each class of grammar needs a kind of machine.

170
00:19:11,783 --> 00:19:22,706
Question 12.3.1. What machine recognizes an L L one language? A pushdown automaton. The table says a deterministic one.

171
00:19:23,156 --> 00:19:40,201
Question 12.3.2. What grammar class is needed for a to the n, b to the n? A context free grammar. L L one is also a correct answer, because S goes to a S b, or a b, is in the L L one subset.

172
00:19:40,651 --> 00:19:52,961
Question 12.3.3. a to the n, b to the n, c to the n needs a context sensitive grammar. Counting three things is outside the context free class.

173
00:19:53,411 --> 00:20:05,357
Question 12.3.6. What can a C F G express that a peg cannot? Ambiguity. Pegs do not permit ambiguity. We study pegs after the midterm.

174
00:20:05,807 --> 00:20:14,149
To summarize. A grammar is ambiguous when one legal string has two parse trees. One example string is the proof.

175
00:20:14,599 --> 00:20:25,628
For precedence, give each level its own nonterminal. The lowest level is at the top. Each level uses the next higher level. The highest level has the operands.

176
00:20:26,078 --> 00:20:35,955
For associativity, a left associative operator needs a left recursive rule, and a right associative operator needs a right recursive rule.

177
00:20:36,405 --> 00:20:45,877
Left recursion is not L L one. Removing it changes the trees. E B N F repetition gives a weakly equivalent grammar.

178
00:20:46,327 --> 00:21:00,066
For the dangling else, end if, a required else, and indentation remove the ambiguity from the grammar. A note in the specification does not. The next video tests if a grammar is L L one.
